In article <id.YS4P.ODL@ferranti.com> peter@ferranti.com (Peter da Silva) writes:
   I've been following this thread for a while. What's to stop some user from
   binary editing a diskette offline to have dangerous files in it (setuid
   files, device files, etc) and mounting it? Why would you want to let users
   have this sort of ability?

Well, you can always disable setuid binaries by setting nosuid when
mounting the floppy in the program that does the mounting.  What we
really need is a "nodev" option to cause special files to be ignored
on a filesystem if it is set when the filesystem is mounted.  Does any
vendor have this already?

Another approach is simply not to care whether or not the users can
mount setuid files, device files, etc.  If your workstations are
dataless, single-user workstations on which superuser access does not
gain the user anything, such as the workstations at Athena, this is
possible.  We've been letting users mount arbitrary filesystems for
years; for that matter, we publish the root password of our public
workstations widely (it's "mrroot").

No, I'm not going to get into the argument, for the nth time, of
whether or not this is a good thing.  It works for us.  If you don't
understand why, read some of the papers that have been published by
Athena; I don't have the energy to go through the argument again.

