(Michael Thomas) writes:
|> In article <1992Apr27.210248.11691@sura.net>, lidl@sura.net (Kurt J.
Lidl) writes:
|> |> "mount" is not privledged under Ultrix.  All you need is ownership of
|> |> the mount point.
|> 
|>    Gack! I just looked at the man page for RiscOS (mips) and SunOS, and
|> they are priviledged commands. (Actually it is mount(2) which requires
|> root) This is a pretty gross security hole!

So read the Ultrix man page too: 

     General users can only mount file systems with certain  res-
     trictions.  For example, the user, other than the superuser,
     performing the  mount  must  own  the  directory  directory.
     Furthermore,  no  users other than the superuser can execute
     setuid or setgid programs on the mounted file  systems.   In
     addition, users other than the superuser cannot access block
     or special character devices such as rra0g  on  the  mounted
     file systems.

In other words, if a general user does the mount, setuid, setgid and device
special files are automatically locked out. Thus it is not a security hole.

