If money was no real problem, would there be any advantage in buying expensive
packages from companies like Digital to connect several PC's and MAC's to a
tcp/ip network (to ftp, telnet, e-mail both ways) instead of using the much
cheaper alternatives available (like pc/nfs LifeLine)?  It seems to me they all
offer virtually the same features.   I hate to pick the cheaper alternatives
(which I don't mind) and later find we would have been better off with the more
expensive products but were unable to purchase them since the money was no
longer available.  On the other hand, it seems silly to spend so much money
when the cheaper products provide just as much functionality and the money
saved could be spent on other things (like extra computers).  The difference in
cost is amazing (especially when support is included) yet the difference in
functionality is little, if any.  I presume installation is pretty much the
same for all (i.e., non-trivial).  Of course we could make pc/mac users telnet
to a mail server in order to send/receive their mail but it's not as elegant
and many of the users would have a hard time just remembering what to do with
unix mail - they would prefer to use a utility on their own computer which has
a better user interface.

Comments please?  WARNING: no flames/wars please - it almost goes without
saying now since I have noticed a somewhat improved state of sanity on the net
of late.  Thanks.

