>"Just say No" is the best option. It is possible on at least some Unixes
>(maybe most, I don't know) to use a 'nosuid' option for mount, which prevents
>any suid programs from doing the suid.

	The problem of a mountable floppy file system can be overcome I think
, I wrote a "mount" command for a floppy that does the following :-

	1) Traps all user generated signals
	2) Gets the real uid of the person running it, if uid=0 then
	   the mounter is root already so no point in worrying
	3) Change owner and group of Floppy device file to "root" and set
	   protection to 700, this prevents later attacks with file system
	   debuggers etc.
	4) Mount the floppy file system.
	5) Change  owner and group of mount point to root and set protection
	   to 700 this stops access while the rest of the checks are carried
	   out.
	6) Scan the floppy for any setuid/setgid files and remove the setuid
	   or setgid bit.
	7) Scan the floppy for any device files, change ownership to root and
	   set protection to 550.
	8) Reset permissions on the mount point to be owned by the mount 
	   requestor with 700 permissions.

	The corresponding unmount command just resets the permissions on the
mount point and device file to appopriate values after unmounting the floppy.

	Since floppy file systems are rarely larger than 2500 blocks max the
overhead of this checking is small.  I think this makes the floppy mount 
command safe, If anybody can think of ways around this I'd be interested. The
source is for a System V based machine, I don't know how portable it is as
I don't have anything else to try it on, I'll mail the source on request.

