We still haven't gotten SIGIO to work when there is data  in  a  pipe,
but  I  do have a stripped-down version of the program that may or may
not hint what the problem is, while disproving all our  hypotheses  so
far.   Our evidence so far is that Ultrix doesn't ever produce a SIGIO
signal when input arrives in a pipe.  I'd love to be proved wrong...

To try the following program, you should "mknod tstpipe p"  to  create
its  input  pipe.   You  can  then  run  it, and it will attempt to do
interrupt-driven input from the pipe.  For input, I have a tiny little
program "jabber.c" that just does:
   n = strlen(av[ac-1]); 
   while(1) {
      write(1,av[ac-1],n); write(1,"\n",1); sleep(1);
   }
and I direct stdout to tstpipe.  This works fine, and  "cat  <tstpipe"
shows a message once per second like you'd expect.

The following "sigtst.c" program doesn't work fine.  What it  does  is
open  tstpipe  for input, and tries to set it up so that when input is
available, sigIO() will be called. It then loops for 10 sec at a time,
and  after  each  sleep(10), it tries to read from the pipe.  You will
notice that the reads work,  which  is  proof  that  data  is  flowing
through  the  pipe  correctly.  You will also notice that if you "kill
-23" the process, sigIO() gets called, proving that the  kernel  knows
about  the SIGIO handler.  But when the above jabbering program writes
to the pipe once a second, sigIO never gets called.  Instead, every 10
seconds,  the  chunk()  call  reads  3 or 4 messages from the pipe and
outputs them, thus proving that the  data  was  there  although  sigIO
wasn't called.

The weird thing is that sigIO() does get called if you kill the writer
to  the  pipe.  This presumably does a close() on the write end, which
should produce an interrupt, and it does.  The program then reads  one
chunk and goes back to the sleep loop, hoping for another sigIO() call
which never comes (although there is usually more data in the pipe).

It appears that we now have proof that 1) The Ultrix 4.2 kernel  knows
how  to  generate a SIGIO signal; 2) It generates this signal properly
when the writer exits; 3) It  doesn't  generate  SIGIO  when  data  is
written to the pipe.

BTW,  someone  (Matt Thomas upstairs, actually) suggested the F_SIGOWN
call. This doesn't fix the problem.  But if you delete that line, then
the interrupts don't happen when the writer exits. This makes no sense
to me at all; fcntl(fil, F_SETOWN, pid) seems like a  really  demented
way  to  say  "interrupt me if a writer to the pipe exits".  I suspect
that it doesn't really mean this, but I haven't a clue  as  to  what's
going on here.  In fact, I don't have a clue as to why F_SETOWN should
even be necessary for this program to work.

Has anyone actually gotten a SIGIO signal to interrupt a program  when
there is new data in a pipe? Can you show me a code fragment that does
it successfully? We have a strong suspicion that we might be the first
people to ever try it on this system.

Anyhow, here's the radically stripped-down program. I've tried various
orders  to the signal() and fcntl() calls, to no avail.  I also have a
debug version on hand with lots of failure  messages,  if  that  would
help any.

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
#include <stdio.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <signal.h>
/*
* This is a test program for the SIGIO signal.  It opens  a  file  and
* sets  up  asynchronous  I/O  on  it, then sits in a loop waiting for
* interrupts. It does a read each time around the loop, to catch cases
* where the kernel fails to send the SIGIO interrupt.  NOTE: This is a
* test program that DOES NOT WORK on Ultrix 4.2; I'm  trying  to  find
* out why.  DON'T use this as a prototype.  IT DOESN'T WORK.
*/
#define BUF 64
char  buf[BUF+1] = {0};
int   bufsiz = BUF;
int   fil = -1;
char *filnam = "tstpipe";
extern int  errno;
extern void sigIO();
/*
* Read a buffer of data and display it.
*/
chunk()
{	int n;
	n = read(fil, buf, bufsiz);
	if (n > 0) {
		buf[n] = 0;
		fputs(buf, stdout);		/* Dump to stdout */
		fflush(stdout);
	} else if (n == 0) {
		errno = 0;	/* This might be our exit status */
	}
	return n;
}
/*
* Input interrupt routine.
*/
void sigIO()
{
	fputs("\nsigIO called.\n",stdout);
	fflush(stdout);
	chunk();
}
main(ac, av, ev)
	char **av, **ev;
{	int  a, n, s;
	int  pid;
	for (a=1; a<ac; a++) {
		switch (av[0][0]) {
		default:
			filnam = av[a];
	}	}
	if ((fil = open(filnam, 0, 0)) < 0) {
		exit(errno);
	}
	signal(SIGIO, sigIO);	/* Declare IO interrupt handler */
	fcntl(fil, F_SETFL, FASYNC);	/* Ask for interrupts on this file */
	pid = getpid();
	fcntl(fil, F_SETOWN, pid);		/* Matt Thomas suggested this */
	while (1) {
		sleep(10);
		chunk();	/* Try to read some data */
	}
	/*NOTREACHED*/
}
- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -


