That policy, if it truly exists, and it certainly didn't at places
I've worked, would only serve to guarantee that money can't be made
by volume.  But that's the whole point of this argument isn't it?
Unix is not a commodity market - if you don't care then you don't
care what happens to NT.  If you think that the continued survival
of Unix depends on its entering the commodity market then you care
a lot.  I see the Unix pundits complaining a lot that the reason that
Unix hasn't taken off is that it doesn't have a killer app (or for
that matter, any large store of commodity apps).  But there is a very
serious chicken and egg problem there, and I don't see a solution
(short of divine intervention :-).

>	4)	Simple tools can be stable.  Complex tools can not.  Ever
>		heard of someone needing to repair a hammer?  An Acuna lathe,
>		on the other hand, requires servicing.  A more apt example
>		of the DOS/UNIX dichotomy might be a skateboard vs a car.
This argument assumes that a) users need the complexity that Unix offers,
and b) that they can't perform complex tasks on their PCs.  I don't believe
that either argument is true for the vast majority of the market.

>	5)	Install scripts differ, again, for vendor differentiation;
>		you can't seriously believe that the vendors currently in
>		business could survive a "level playing field"?!?
I don't know if they could or not, but I don't think many of them are
going to survive the current playing field.

>	6)	Technology benefits extensively through diversity.  What was
>		the last technical breakthrough for PC machines that resulted
>		from a niche market demand?  PC vendors are in it for volume
>		and are not niche-market driven.
Back to the commodity argument again, but in any case...  The Mac
has done a number of things that were "niche" driven - desktop
publishing, multi-media development, CD-ROM usage.  Many of those
have then appeared in the next generation of PC products.  Unix
machines have only very high-end versions of those products, but
it's notable that those high-end companies have all come out with
(equally high-end) PC versions.

>	It sounds more like you just got bit by having UNIX machines from
>two different vendors and expecting them to act exactly the same.  This is
I have four different Unix vendors' workstations on my desk.  I never
expected them to be exactly the same; I did expect them to be paying
more than lipservice to that goal.  I was wrong.  This is great if you
are in the system integration business, it's lousy if you're in the
software business.

>similar to buying a PC and a Macintosh and expecting the same thing.  It is
Ah, and therein lies the problem.  The customer knows that if they buy
a Mac they are stuck with Apple.  But if they buy a PC they can switch
vendors all they want and still run the same OS and same apps.  But
they are limited - it's all basically the same hardware, and maybe it
doesn't do everything they need in terms of speed and OS features.  Now
along comes Unix to the rescue.  But now they discover (as you say)
that Unix is "snake oil".  They are no more free to switch platforms
easily than if they had bought a Mac.  Then along comes NT.  It claims
to have the power of Unix, and the compatiblity of DOS.  If it can do
half of what they claim those users will choose it over Unix.

Now you can argue that you don't care about those customers.  I can
buy that argument (and I think to a certain extent that's what you
are saying - that Unix doesn't belong in the commodity market).  I
don't like that argument though, and I don't think that Sun, HP, DEC
or IBM are building their businesses based on the assumption that
Unix has lost the war for the desktop - so if it *has* lost that
war I think you can look for some major upsets.

Frankly I've talked to people dealing with strategy at a number of
hardware companies and although many are trying last ditch defenses
against NT (usually by trying to come out with a low cost machine
bundled with lots of handy commodity software) they pretty much
conceed that they don't have much hope past the next three to five
years.  Sun and USL don't seem to have given up yet, but elsewhere
I think the battle is shifting to the corporate server market - the
desktop has been conceeded to the the PCs and Macs.

>not possible to hide all architectural differences between two machines under
>a single blanket called "UNIX", nor is it possible to hide differences in
>software which in many cases are intentional (ala Tandy DOS not booting on
>non-Tandy machines.
First of all most software never touches the hardware - we don't
have a single #ifdef based on machine architecture (well, except
byte order, which is pretty trivial).  Secondly I don't buy this
conspiracy theory of "we have to do it different".  I have many
times seen "we have to do it better", which often gets translated
into the same thing.  But I don't see people going out of their
way to be different - they just don't know how to go about making
it the same.  OSF was an attempt.  Unix International was another.
Neither seems to have done any good.



