In article <PRZEMEK.92Apr21131603@rrdstrad.nist.gov> przemek@rrdstrad.nist.gov (Przemek Klosowski) writes:
: How about writing "configure" in Perl and writing a sed script (or sh
: or yacc) to translate it into a shell script? The hard parts are the
: rules and special cases, aren't they? 

It would have to written in a strict subset of Perl to have much hope
of doing that.  It might be easier to maintain two separate versions of
Configure than to write a Perl to sh translator.  Presumably one could
send both versions if there was any doubt about the availability of
Perl.  (It wouldn't be difficult to modify metaconfig to spit out either
version.)

The main problem with Configure is that it can never be totally correct
unless we can convince vendors to stop changing their products, which
is of course a hopeless task.  There's always a new place to look for
libraries, a new format for library filenames, a new format for the
output of nm, a new set of cpp symbols that you have to define to make
things work like they used to, a new set of switches to the C compiler
or the loader, a new set of optimizations that break the code in subtle
ways, a new set of library routines of which some are actually
implemented, a new set of documents to read before you have any idea
what the heck is going on...

It makes you want to sympathize with the folks who say "Just punt and
hire a guru to do all your installations."

It's not that I'm against job security for gurus.  :-)  It's just that
there are a lot of people out there who want *me* to be their guru, and
I tend to run a wee bit short on time.  I wrote Configure (and metaconfig)
purely in self-defense.  (Likewise for patch.)  By putting a bit of
expert system out there, I save myself a lot of work.  But the way things
are diverging, it's taking more and more work to save work.  Sigh.
Everything is a tradeoff.

Well, I've wandered from the topic.  But I feel much better now...

Larry Wall
lwall@netlabs.com


