Lines: 56

In article <sherman.704002424@lea>, sherman@lea.csc.ncsu.edu (Chris Sherman) writes:
> I have a question about vmstat which, surprising, the man page doesn't cover.

See if you can find a copy of the "Reader's Comments" page and fill
it out.

> 
> When I do a 'vmstat -v 5', I get the following output:
> 
>       procs            memory
>   r dw pw sl  w   vm   avm  rm  arm  fre
>   1  0  1  8  0   34k  10k 5664 3196  872
> 
> The man page does no tell us what dw, pw, sl, w, vm, rm, nor arm
> are.

These are counted for processes whose status is SSLEEP or SSTOP.

dw - A process with the SLOAD flag set and the priority <= PZERO.  Also
     called Disk Wait, hence the dw.
pw - A process with the SPAGE flag set.
sl - Sleeping.  Mostly the ones not in Disk Wait.
w  - In the manual page ("runnable or short sleeper, but swapped")

The remaining words in your list are virtual memory related.

vm - Total Virtual memory.  This is the sum of text, stack and data
     spaces for all processes.
rm - Total Real memory.  The sum of text, stack and data resident
     set sizes for all processes.
avm - Active virtual memory.  The total of stack and data spaces
      of those process with status SRUN or SIDL.

> 
> Is there a way to read this an ask how much virtual memory
> is left for me to use?

You have as much Virtual memory to use as you have page/swap
space to back it up.  You want to look at pstat(8) and the -s
option to find that.  Given the information in the -v listing
of vmstat(1) one other little bit of information you can get a
good guess at much real memory you can use before you start
paging.  This other little bit of information is "lotsfree"
which is the amount of memory the kernel tries to keep free
when there isn't enough.

See Also: The "Performance Management Guide" in the V4.2A documentation
	  set.  The part number is AA-PKDVA-TE if you want to order one.

> 
> Thanx!

> -- Chris Sherman
