In article <1992Apr13.180423.29110@mits.mdata.fi>, karttu@mits.mdata.fi (Antti Karttunen) writes:
> Currently I have the PDP-11/23+ with the four 10 MB RL02-drives and
> 2MB of RAM. It is running the RSX-11M version 4.? (or RSX-11M-PLUS 2.?).
> However, I would like to have something little more effective for the
> mass storage. So I'm thinking about purchasing the SCSI-controller
> for the beast. Some questions:
> 
> 1) Is it possible for the PDP-11/23-PLUS?

  Yes. A number of companies make Q-bus SCSI controllers. Some of the more
popular ones are Dilog (SQ700 series), Emulex (UC series) and CMD.

> 2) Are there any used ones (controllers) available in U.S.A?
>    In this part of Europe? Prices?

  Probably. I've seen them now and again, usually in comp.sys.dec or misc.
forsale.computers.

> 3) Hardest problem: The software. Is there any device drivers for SCSI
>    available in RSX-11M-(???) ? Do I have to do the SYSGEN to get the
>    system know the SCSI-disk? (I have _no_ sysgen packs! :-()
>    How hard it's to hack the OS to boot from the SCSI-disk, and not use
>    the RL02's at all?

  Nobody puts un-emulated SCSI on the Q-bus any more. The controllers you
will find today emulate one of the DEC device types, normally MSCP - the
DU device. This is because MSCP is self-sizing, while the older types needed
patches to reflect the actual size and geometry of your SCSI disk.

  As far as the boot goes, depending on the age of your quad-width 11/23 CPU,
you may or may not have DU bootstrap support. You could always order the DEC
kit KDF11-B3, which consists of two new ROMs to upgrade that board to a Micro-
PDP-11 with full bootstrap support for the newer devices. I think it costs
about US $100.

  I think you'll need to do some sort of configure/sysgen to get RSX to see
the new disks - but I'm not an RSX person.

