ckd@eff.org (Christopher Davis) writes:

}The only place I've seen "prevent people from logging into a workstation
}that's in use" was MIT Athena, and that comes from limitations of
}Kerberos more than from the need to secure displays.

They aren't the only place, and no, it isn't a limitation of Kerberos.
It is the realization that physical access to a system subverts
security.  Until vendors come up with a way to secure the "rom
monitor" this will continue to be the case.  DEC gets the "we almost
have it now" award for having a password you can set to restrict access
to "rom monitor" commands -- except they provide no way to secure the
case -- a quick thumbscrew, pull the battery leads, and, voila, no more
password -- if you're lucky that is, if you're not they steal the disk :-(

John


