A little background:

   EWS boots off of a VAX/VMS, VAX/Ultrix or RISC/Ultrix machine like a terminal
   server, makes a connection back to the host that booted it (usually through
   DECnet on the VAX/VMS systems) and loads specific configuration information,
   then exists on the network as an independant node.  A menu comes up with
   machines (either DECnet or TCP/IP) and when one of the machines is "clicked"
   on, a port (either DECnet or TCP/IP) is tickled and a digital banner and
   login window is thrown up on the workstation by the foreign host.  All
   windows run from the one host or any other hosts allowed to display on the 
   workstation;  no windows run on the workstation.

We have had a lot of success with the EWS Package with the following notes:

   o  The VAX/VMS installation documentation leaves something (actually MANY
      somethings) to be desired.  There are a number of errors in this doc and
      you should contact your DEC service person/DECdirect about the changes
      (or contact me... I have figured all of them out) (Ever set an account
      parameter too high? EWS needs one in a range.  Too high == not work. 
      Too low == not work.  Very frustrating)

   o  We have been unable to get a login window to appear by making a TCP/IP
      connection to a VAX/VMS machine (we have Multinet TCP/IP software).

   o  In order to make a connection (login) straight to a host, the host must be
      running the "host" portion of the EWS software.  EWS software is available
      for VAX/VMS, VAX/Ultrix and RISC/Ultrix systems. 

Most of our problems were with the installtion instructions.  After we got over
that hurtle, everything works great (including the TCP/IP transport into the
workstation).  Also, we are still waiting on the Ultrix software to some in.

Some of the advantages:

   o  One of your workstations goes down with a system disk failure... you can 
      boot it with EWS and still use it while waiting for DEC service to arrive.
      No time lost waiting (assuming you have your files somewhere else).

   o  Makes VAXstation 2000's with an RD53 (of which we have a literal ton)
      useful again (RD53 is 71MBytes which cannot hold VMS 5.4-3 (or >) and
      DECwindows or Ultrix 4.2 (or >)).  Also, VAXstation 2000's are REAL quiet
      with their drive disconnected (I can give someone instructions as to how
      to do this... the innerds of a VS2000 are a little tight.  This could
      void your service contract, make you shake, make you go blind and make 
      hair grow on your palms).

EWS works for VAXstation II/GPX's, Monochrome VS3100's & VS2000's,  Color
VS3100's & VS2000's, VAXstation 3100/SPX's, and VT1300 X Terminals (the old
ones).

I do not think the new VXT2000 software supports the VAXstations (but I don't
know for sure).

