Lines: 59



Within Philips we currently use DEC's DDS directory service - which
works adequately for queries. However, batch updating is a major
performance issue!  To explain our problem:

We currently use computers from several vendors and with different
operating systems (MVS, VM, VMS, UNIXes, MS-DOS, etc). Each platform
uses its own email system and there are some gateways between these
platforms.

To keep track of where users are located and some data about these
persons, a network directory is maintained per country or geographical
area for the IBM email users. Every month, these directory files are
exchanged and the new files replace the previous version. At present
we have about 25 of these IBM or IBM-compatible computers with each
their own directory database for a total of over 50000 users.

For the DEC VAX/VMS users there are a number of DDS Message Routers.
We have one master node which is also the only "WORLD SEARCH NODE" and
several caching nodes (> 10). The DDS contains data about users of
DECmail, ALL-IN-1 and X400.  Data about the IBM users is also included
in the DDS-database. This is where the problem occurs. The IBM
directories are sent to the DDS master node once per month. On the DDS
node an update request is queued for every user for whom data has
been modified (deleted, added). This can amount to 10000 update
requests.  To process these requests the message router needs about 5
to 7 days :-(. Apparently the reason is the slow queueing and
synchronizing of databases over the network according to DEC. DEC
proposes a custom solution to be developed for Philips - but we
somehow feel that a generic or fundamental issue is at hand. While we
appreciate that DDS was not developed for such large amount of batch
changes - we do feel that this is common enough problem to want a
generic solution.

OK, we do accept the criticism that we should try to avoid the once a
month large batch run, but that requires an inordinate amount of
organizational coordination by different groups in various countries
 -  a technical solutions seems more cost-effective. 

Does anyone else have the same or similar problem? If so, have you
found a solution or are you working on one? What would you recommend
to minimize the update times?

If you have a large DDS directory (more than 50000 entries)
geographically distributed could you indicate how updating and
synchronization is done? Any comments on DDS performance and usage?

Thank you for your effort,

regards,

