>Warning: fsck -y can easily mangle your disks badly.

Well, I wouldn't say "easily".  It's a theoretical possibility, most
likely to happen after some hardware failure has caused file system
damage.  In all the years that we ran fsck -y on V7 systems (utzoo and
utcsstat, notably), I can't recall any file system problems made worse by
fsck -y.

The temptation to use fsck -y these days is probably the result of
increasingly wimpy fsck -p implementations.  I've seen fsck -p scream
"UNEXPECTED INCONSISTENCY" over a zero-length directory.  Yeah, it's
unusual; either remove it or add . and .., but quit yer bitchin' and get
on with it.
