In article <BMxnoH.BJM@world.std.com>, geoff@world.std.com (Geoff Collyer) writes:
|> Chris Siebenmann:
|> >Warning: fsck -y can easily mangle your disks badly.
|> 
|> Well, I wouldn't say "easily".  It's a theoretical possibility, most
|> likely to happen after some hardware failure has caused file system
|> damage.  In all the years that we ran fsck -y on V7 systems (utzoo and
|> utcsstat, notably), I can't recall any file system problems made worse by
|> fsck -y.o

*I* have!  We lost a whole bunch of home directories (for reasons I'd
rather not explain).  Unfortunately, fsck isn't smart enough to reattach
subdirectories in tact, so every file in the entire tree had to be
reattached (placed in lost+found).  It didn't take long before lost+found
filled up!  Remember that fsck CAN'T extend the length of the lost+found
directory.  My only options were (1) let fsck remove the files and (2)
leave them dangling (by answer no to all the questions), mount the file
system, move files out of lost+found, then rerun fsck.  Option 1 wasn't
much of an option.  I chose option 2.  It was a looooong night.

|> The temptation to use fsck -y these days is probably the result of
|> increasingly wimpy fsck -p implementations.  I've seen fsck -p scream
|> "UNEXPECTED INCONSISTENCY" over a zero-length directory.

Agreed.  There are some pretty lame things that fsck -p doesn't want to
fix.  But I would never do an fsck -y in /etc/rc.  And I would be very
reluctant to do one even manually (unless I really had a good reason and
I was convinced that it wouldn't cause any serious problems).

