In article <1992Apr6.034323.27991@alphalpha.com>, nazgul@alphalpha.com (Kee Hinckley) writes:
|> In article <1992Apr1.211848.21179@dsd.es.com> pmartz@bambam.dsd.es.com (Paul Martz) writes:
|> >In article <1992Apr1.161010.11988@auto-trol.com>, marbru@auto-trol.com (Martin Brunecky) writes:
|> >>   The end user is tired of Unix promises. He wants stability, not 
|> >>   a permanent revolution.
|> >
|> >The same thing could be said of the PC world. First CPM, then early
|> >revs of DOS, then DOS 3.0 (finally something useable), DOS 5.0, the
|> >Presentation Manager, now Windows NT... You call this stability?
|> More stable than 15 different Unix platforms with 15 different sets
|> of #ifdefs and 15 different install scripts and 15 different types
|> of customer support questions, and in many cases even more stable
|> than two sucessive releases on the same Unix platform.

	This is unfair:

	1)	DOS runs on only one platform
	2)	Most #ifdef's are a sign of poor programming if they are
		used for per-machine portability.  This is generally not
		the case.  #ifdef's generally refer to major architectural
		differences and refer to a class of machines rather than a
		single machine.
	3)	UNIX is not a commodity market.  DOS is.  You don't see a
		zillion Pyramid/Mips/Vax knock-offs out there.  UNIX hardware
		isn't commodity at all.  UNIX itself is generally MADE at
		least slightly incompatible between boxes to differentiate
		vendors and to insure a user and his peripherals are locked
		into a single vendor.  This is because money can't be made
		by volume (see MicroChannel).
	4)	Simple tools can be stable.  Complex tools can not.  Ever
		heard of someone needing to repair a hammer?  An Acuna lathe,
		on the other hand, requires servicing.  A more apt example
		of the DOS/UNIX dichotomy might be a skateboard vs a car.
	5)	Install scripts differ, again, for vendor differentiation;
		you can't seriously believe that the vendors currently in
		business could survive a "level playing field"?!?
	6)	Technology benefits extensively through diversity.  What was
		the last technical breakthrough for PC machines that resulted
		from a niche market demand?  PC vendors are in it for volume
		and are not niche-market driven.

	It sounds more like you just got bit by having UNIX machines from
two different vendors and expecting them to act exactly the same.  This is
similar to buying a PC and a Macintosh and expecting the same thing.  It is
not possible to hide all architectural differences between two machines under
a single blanket called "UNIX", nor is it possible to hide differences in
software which in many cases are intentional (ala Tandy DOS not booting on
non-Tandy machines.


					Terry Lambert
					terry@icarus.weber.edu

These opinions are my own and are not attributable to my employer.


