Hi again Mark,
	Well, do you specifically need a WORM drive?  Or could you
settle for an erasable optical drive?  Yes, WORMS store more (~5 times)
but usually the cost/nonerasability factor is a consideration.  There
are many SCSI (small computer systems interface) Floptical drives on the
market currently.  They use a standard 3.5" floppy, and most drives are
compatible with eachother.  Each disk stores ~128MB o data, and is
entirely erasable/rewriteable.  They cost ~1500 currently, and if you
shop around (check out 800 numbers in any Mac mag) probably cheaper.
Also, disks run about $50 apiece, or so I am told.  Again, shopping will
probably save you a few bucks, but then again, you are most likely on
some research grant, and $$$ arent an issue! ;)  
	As for compatibility... I am 99.99% sure that some sort of SCSI
port either exists or some card/interface/doohickey-thingamabob exists
fairly inexpensively.  SCSI is pretty neat because it allows a bunch (up
to 7 on macs) of devices, ranging from hard drives to Bernoulli drives,
to floptical drives to cd-rom,etc , to be connected in a chain to one
port on the computer.
