*************************************************************
Subject: RE: Digitizier input to a Decstation 5000
Date: Tue, 07 Apr 92 09:42:15 +0200
From: Stefan Manke <manke@ira.uka.de>
Yes, you are right: /dev/tty00 should be the right device driver for port 2 at your
DECstation (I use my WACOM digitizer at port 3 with /dev/tty01.)
I haven't made any changes in /etc/ttys.
My source code for opening the digitizer for read/write looks as follows:
	
static int OpenRS232C (device, baudrate)
char *device;
int baudrate;
{
  int fd, i;
  struct sgttyb portstat;

   /* open device */
   if ((fd = open (device, O_RDWR, 0)) < 0) {
     /* unable to open device */
     fprintf (stderr, "!!! error: can't open RS232C device \"%s\"\n", device);
     return -1;
   }
   else { /* device now is open */
     /* set baud rate and transmission mode of device */
     portstat.sg_ispeed = portstat.sg_ospeed = baudrate;
     portstat.sg_flags = RAW|ANYP;
     if (ioctl (fd, TIOCSETP, &portstat) == -1) {
       fprintf (stderr, "!!! error: ioctl TIOCSETP\n");
       return -1;
     }
     else {
       return fd;
     }
   }
}

where device is defined as "/dev/tty01" and baudrate is B19200.
Don't forget to set the corresponding baudrate at your digitizer!
Just a hint: Have you set the file protection mode of /dev/tty00 to it's right value?
In your particular case it should be crw-r--r-- or crw-rw-rw-. By default it has
crw--w--w- .
