disks to less than 2 ** 21 LBNs was fixed.  The symtom was that
I/O past that magic number would wrap around to the beginning of
the disk.  It would tend to look like disk corruption.  Look around
for the ULTRIX FAQ, it may be mentioned there.

> 
> Oops, Ultrix 4.1 doesn't support the rz58.  Neither does 4.2.
> You'll need to upgrade to at least 4.2A to run one of those.

I've had occasion to use unannounced and unsupported disks on
ULTRIX before.  If you don't mind the autoconfiguration phase
reporting the disk as RZxx, the error log report generator not 
knowing the quirks of the disk's error log messages, having to
construct a disktab entry by hand (See Also: creatediskbyname(3))
and other assorted minor hassles UNSUPPORTED doesn't necessarily
mean doesn't work.  Sometimes it does of course; trying to use
an RZ58 on ULTRIX V4.0 would definately encounter that SCSI LBN
limit.

On recent versions of ULTRIX, SCSI disks are easier to deal with
than new RA or RF disks.  And even those aren't hard if you can
find out what the media ID is.

Support Disclaimer: If you are trying to use an unsupported disk
on some version of ULTRIX and call the CSC to complain don't get
bent out of shape if they say, "That's not supported.  Please upgrade
to ULTRIX Vx.y".

