In article <1992Apr3.052637.3357@yrloc.ipsa.reuter.COM> rbe@yrloc.ipsa.reuter.COM (Robert Bernecky) writes:
>In article <1992Mar30.170256.11410@vax5.cit.cornell.edu> umh@vax5.cit.cornell.edu writes:
>>> On the other hand, one could say that crashme is the moral equivalent of
>>> jabbing a screwdriver into the innards of your Walkman and then
>>> complaining that it wasn't built sturdily enough.
>
>Patient:   "Doctor, it hurts when I do this."
>
>Doctor:    "Well, don't do that."

I think both analogies are flawed.  Here's my version of those
analogies that are more appropriate:

  Crashme is the moral equivalent of turning the dials on your Walkman real
  fast and discovering that it stops working when you do that.

Note that the difference is that you're doing operations that are supposed
to work, but just in a way that the designer didn't anticipate.

This version highlights the issue that computers are often used by several
people, and one person doesn't have control over what the others do.  In
particular, there's no way to prevent them from executing broken sequences
of instructions.

If you don't like to think of users sharing a system as adversaries,
here's a variant:

  Patient: Doctor, I bleed when I accidentally get hit by a stray bullet.

  Doctor: Wear a bulletproof vest.

The computer equivalent of the "stray bullet" is a legitimate program that
just happens to execute a broken instruction sequence.

While it's not reasonable for everyone to wear bulletproof vests just in
case a shootout happens to break out near them, it *is* reasonable to
expect CPU manufacturers to fix these kinds of problems.  Note, however,
that I didn't say that it's reasonable to expect CPUs not to have these
kinds of bugs; CPU architecture has many similarities to software design,
and I know how hard it is to write completely bug-free programs (basically,
if the program is of reasonable complexity, it's virtually impossible).
