In article <id.QTJO.41A@ferranti.com> peter@ferranti.com (Peter da Silva) writes:
>In article <1992Apr2.021133.19228@riacs.edu> lamaster@viking.arc.nasa.gov (Hugh LaMaster -- RCS) writes:
>> This seems to me to be mixing apples and oranges.  Every version of Unix that
>> I have worked with over the last few years was stable when asked to do the
>> kinds of things which standalone PCs and Macs do.  And most, in fact, were
>> stable doing almost anything.
>
>And if they cost about the same as the PCs and Macs, and if you could get
>application software for them at Babbages that was as cheap and as good as
>the stuff you can get for the PCs and Macs, then they'd totally flatten them
>in the marketplace.

Sure, my PC crashes more frequently than my Unix box, but when it does, it
only takes 15 seconds to reboot, not 15 minutes.  I'm willing to put up with
that to have a REALLY GOOD editor (brief or sage or ...) that I can afford
($250 or less), a REALLY GOOD word processor (WordPerfect 5.1 or Word or ...)
that I can afford (~$500), a REALLY GOOD Fortran development system with a
REALLY FAST compiler and REALLY GOOD debugger that knows Fortran, not just C
(Lahey), a REALLY GOOD spread sheet, data base, ...

>> As far as applications software, there seems to be a wealth, an abundance, of
>> Unix software now, and in fact, a lot of it has X interfaces - spreadsheets,
>> project management, configuration management and control, publishing, pretty
>> much you-name-it.
>
>OK, you have $2000. Go buy a UNIX box, hardware, software, applications, and
>a 24-pin printer.

The problem with Unix applications is that those one can afford are really
sleazy (e.g. vi -- one level undo? edit one file at a time? modeful interface?
get real!) or really sloppy (e.g. emacs takes 37 MB of disk space, brief fits
in one MB), while the good ones are really expensive (compare Frame or
Interleaf or Island Write or Unix WordPerfect to Wordperfect or Word on a PC).

>> Everyone agrees that a standalone Unix system is harder to administer than a
>> standalone Mac or PC.
>
>Well, mainly, when it breaks it breaks in less obvious ways. I'll agree that
>the network situation is exactly the opposite: UNIX tends to let you know
>what went wrong less ambiguously.

Maybe when YOUR workstation breaks some network related thing your Unix
tells you more obviously what is going on.  I recently had just the
opposite experience:  (1)  The sendmail.cf that was delivered with our
system carefully stripped off the internet domain name.  (2) since it
was sendmail.cf, which is the only thing I've ever seen that's worse than
OS\360 JCL, I just got one from a colleague, and it seemed to work.  One
doesn't look inside of sendmail.cf unless absolutely necessary, and one
has almost no hope of changing it in such a way as to improve it.
(3) A gateway two hops away broke.  (4) Nothing in my system told me that
gateway was broken, since there was another gateway that gave me access
to the same places.  (5) After a while (four weeks), I concluded that
outgoing mail was disappearing down a black hole, even though mail was
still arriving.  (6) After another two weeks I discovered that outgoing
mail was being sent to the broken gateway no matter what the address --
I stumbled onto /usr/spool/mqueue/syslog -- Isn't that an obvious place
to look?  After all, that's where all the other log files for all the
other tools go, right?  (7) After that, it was only a day's work to
discover that the node my mail was being sent to was broken (ping +
lots of phone calls), and eventually discover that it was mentioned
in sendmail.cf as "DAfoo.bar" with nearby comments that "this is your
internet mail relay..." with nothing nearby or in "man sendmail" that
gives you a clue that that's the ONLY place your mail gets sent, even if
it's on the same subnet you're on!

I posted questions on the internet, and didn't get even one suggestion --
apparently, nobody knew even where to start.  (The only way I was able
to post was to log in to another machine).

I don't have anything on my PC's except telnet, rlogin, ftp and nfs,
so I don't know whether network related problems are bigger on PC's
or smaller, but the above experience convinced me that Unix network
problems can get arbitrarily bad.
-- 
Van Snyder = vsnyder@math.jpl.nasa.gov


