petav@Physik.TU-Muenchen.DE (Peter Averkamp) writes:

>I was into that one for some time now, but talking to our service rep.
>only produced the usual garbage ("this might cause data loss...bla,bla..").
>I'm still convinced that I WANT to take the 'risk' to get appropriate
>write performance, as I have to get it for an image processing application.
>So I tried to report it to Dec as a sort of bug, but you can guess the
>answer... 

	A working protocol is not a bug. I agree that it should be a
configurable option, that defaults to "off", but presently that's not
something that's in the works. In other words, if you want to run asynch
NFS you'll have to break the kernel yourself. It's a parameter passed to
the underlying filessytem write routine - you might be able to patch
it yourself with a debugger. (it's in rfs_write())

>Ok, next I tried to at least start an SPR on it, but same (*!X@=inappropriate,
>in my opinion) reaction, although this is not yet finished.

	You're probably wasting your time and ours, unfortunately. If I
could change it, I would have a long time ago.

>Maybe, if enough people complain, we'll get an async mount option in 
>somewhat like 4.2c ? I personnaly do not really believe in it, as this 
>might let the sales on prestoserve drop significantly.

	4.2c will have significant improvements to how ULTRIX does
I/O. Pretty much *all* filesystem throughput will increase. NFS will
still be slow(er) but the reason for our not shipping with asynch
NFS is *NOT* to keep from cannibalizing presto sales. It's a decision
that was made before presto existed, and presently there's just not
a great likelihood we'll see a supported asynch NFS work its way
in to the product. If you're going to agitate, agitate for having it
in OSF/1.

>   2) Could anyone from DEC comment on the legal situation for 
>      exchanging kernel OBJECT files between license owners ?

	I wouldn't put it out for anyonymous FTP, but slipping it
under the table is probably going to go undetected.

	Let me just be a devil's advocate for a second. If you have
problems with your system - things start to act weird. Maybe you
have a few crashes and your clients start to get flaky. Do you
have a software support contract? Will you *honestly* tell Atlanta
you're running an unsupported configuration so they won't waste any
time on you? That's the *reason* there's no asynch NFS, basically.
There are reasons the protocol is synchronous (to make it slow!;) )
and having it be that way makes it more reliable and less likely
to produce weird data integrity problems. Wierd data integrity
problems are *hell* on customer support, and that's why there's this
notion of "supported"/"unsupported" configurations. It lets us draw
a line and say "if you're going to say you don't care about data integrity,
in return you should let us say we can wash our hands of you, support
wise."  Does that sound like a deal? Doesn't to me.  I don't know
how vendors like Sun, who don't even bother to check to make sure
UDP packets are clean ("write garbage and say it's good data") can
get away with it.

mjr.
