In article <1992Apr2.215945.6571@cs.hw.ac.uk> sfleming@cs.hw.ac.uk writes:
>Chuq von Rospach reported a few years ago that he used to
>test Mac programs by allowing his pet cockatoo to walk on
>the keyboard.  It is quite amazing what random input will
>do to "solid" utilities.
>
At one place we had a person in tech support who was good at finding
bugs in the terminal I/O routines of our UNIX product. It turned out
that she was literally rolling her fists back and forth over a
terminal keyboard to see what would happen. The down side of this kind
of testing is that, even if it does find that SOMETHING went wrong, it
is frequently impossible to recreate what it was. We had to spend
weeks searching for bugs nobody was able to recreate. I don't mean to
say we should have shipped product when we knew something was wrong,
but to point out that you get no clues on where to look from some of
these tests!
