From article <30532@skye.dcs.ed.ac.uk>, by dhd@dcs.ed.ac.uk (Damon Hart-Davis):
> In article <r580rINNciv@agate.berkeley.edu>, jbuck@forney.berkeley.edu (Joe Buck) writes:
>> I strongly recommend that test engineers test the effect of executing random
>> code on all CPU/OS combinations.
> 
> What byte sequences do you suggest we start with.
> 
> 0x00
> 
> 0x00 0x00
> 
> 0x00 0x00 0x00
> 
> (for processors where 0x00 is not a NOP of course!)
> 
> This is not a flame, but just HOW do you systematically test for spurious errors. 
> Better to try to avoid them with formal h/w & s/w design.
> 
> Damon

One cannot deny that correctness in design is best.  Presumably
designers do not seek to make mistakes.  And all testing is
subject to the objection that one can never show correctness thereby.
But by hitting a processor with a stream of filtered, randomly chosen
sequences, one sometimes uncovers interactions not allowed for by
the designers.  I have seen it.  That this kind of testing requires
careful study of processor internals is probably no surprise.
Whether it is, in general, useful is probably unanswerable:  it has
to be tried.  I, too, favor scalpels over sledgehammers--but good
engineering is both pragmatic and elegant.

Brian Clausing


