some observations

1) you (well at least we do) use *any* method that's available to find
bugs in design so we have people using formal methods and others using
traditional testing techniques and i'm sure both approaches benefit
the design

2) using formal methods on an existing architecture and implementation
may automatically put it at a disadvantage ... post hoc verification is
generally much less efficient that verification with/guided design

3) when applying formal methods to the t800 fpu ucode i found a bug that
could only have been found by testing by evaluating

	(x * 0) + y, where abs(y) < abs(x)

how long 'till a random code sequence gets that?


