Hi, here is what I have on the VS4000 sound. I have a VLC and have a 
small amplified speaker hooked up to it.  DEC is working on a package
called DECsound that should be available shortly and this promises to
provide all kinds of abilities for sound.

To connect the driver, use the following
$ !
$ run sys$system:sysgen
connect soa0 /adapter=0 /csr=%x0e00 /vector=%o304 /driver=sodriver
exit

Sound files can be found at the following sites (and lots more)

sciences.sdsu.edu   /pub/sounds
solaria.cc.gatech.edu   /pub/sounds and /pub/sounds/ucsd

Here is a message that appeared recently ...

=======================================================================
The only VAXstations currently containing audio as part of the base
product are the VS4000 Model 60 and the VS4000 VLC.  The model 60 
includes a small internal speaker and both can accept an external 
speaker and microphone or headphones.  The codec is part of the base
system and doesn't need to be purchased separately.

The codec chip used is an 8 KHz sample rate mu-law codec (Am79C30A).
The driver for this chip is included with VMS V5.5, but there is no
SYSGEN autoconfigure support in that release, so you need to load it
by hand.  There is also no documentation, but the documetation is 
being updated for VMS V5.5-1.  You can get output from the chip using
the simple DCL command

	$ COPY file.au SO:

A sound recording/editor tool to use with this hardware/software
combination will be available in the not too distant future.


