Tell me---the trade publications that you read---what planet are they
published on?

Page 1 of Open Systems Today for March 30, 1992, contains criticisms
of both Unix and NT in a nice graphic. 


Page 3, the quote of the week: "The idea that open systems will make
you vendor-independent is one of those glorious neophyte
fictions."---the Pentagon's Paul Strassman.

Page 4: NeXT computer looking to adopt standards, users gripe about it
being difficult to connect.

Page 5: Speculation by analysts that Silicon Graphics bought Mips to
lock control over the RX000 architecture.

Flipping pages, on page 10, Nina Lytton's column contains speculation
that---no matter WHAT the vendors are saying---the ACE initiative may
soon ditch the Mips architecture entirely.

Flipping some more pages, over to the end of the issue, we find the
current edition of net.views---one which I'd like to claim credit for,
but I can't, this one has been in the pipeline from when Rob Hertzberg
was still doing them---in which readers were ask how they would spend
$100,000 to bring open systems to companies who are primarily into
proprietary systems. My favorite responses: "Hire a consultant for
$100,000 who would tell them they don't have enough money to buy into
open systems" ... the reader who suggested nuclear weapons be used
against vendors of proprietary systems ... "I'd spend $90,000 on an
intensive one-week top-echelon seminar in a nice `open' area such as,
for example, a beachfront hotel in Hawaii.... " and, best of all,
"Spend it on posters, mugs, pens, T-shirts and a lavish party, all of
which announce `Company X is now Using Open Systems.' At least, that's
what certain manufacturers of `open systems' believe is the method for
moving to open systems."

My favorite piece illustrating OST's editorial integrity was not
recent. To tell you the truth, it wasn't even that big a story. But a
few months ago, we did a piece criticizing an advertisement being run
by Sun. That advertisement appeared in our own publication.

Sorry, guy. Whatever else you care to criticize our publicatio for,
when you hit on our editorial integrity, you're hitting on our strong
point. We're honest with the readers. I go home each night
with a clean conscience.

     


